     \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nEarlier part 9:\n\n$\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2e^x+12x^2-\\frac{x^4}{2}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nCurrent part:\n\nConsider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}-x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x)\n$$\n\nThe order of this differential equation is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." 13194139534183 true] [17592186281097 1113 #uuid "49a8fef0-9677-5fe9-b194-d6a00296e3f9" 13194139534183 true] [17592186281097 1145 "We have the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{3}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}-x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ which has order $4.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $4.$" 13194139534183 true] [17592186281098 1000 #edb/ref 17592186281099 13194139534183 true] [17592186281098 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534183 true] [17592186